FCC filings reveal the designs of three upcoming LG smart home accessories that Apple reportedly helped develop, including a thermostat, a temperature sensor, and a deadbolt lock.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/Smart-Thermostat-Perspective-Feature.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1069141" /><br/>
Yesterday, <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2026/10/06/lg-apple-smart-home-products/"><em>Bloomberg</em> reported</a> that Apple is working with LG Electronics on accessories for its upcoming home hub and other home products, citing regulatory documents without sharing the filings. FCC records <em>MacRumors</em> found contain label-placement drawings, three-dimensional renderings, and technical reports, providing a first look at the products' housings and additional details about their capabilities. <br/>
<br/>
The <a href="https://fccid.io/2BO3L-TMCTAW5W/Label/TMCTAW5W-Label-location-9609590">smart thermostat</a>, model TMCTAW5W, has a round, puck-like body with a raised edge. The drawing shows its back, with a small socket near the center and two round bumps near the bottom. Its specifications list a 24-volt AC power supply.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/Smart-Thermostat-Feature.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1069128" /><br/>
The Matter logo and QR setup code sit in the middle of the back, with regulatory text printed in a circle around them. A separate drawing shows the wiring connections arranged in two curved rows, with one on each side. LG's logo appears in the printing instructions.<br/>
<br/>
The thermostat supports 2.4 GHz and 5 GHz Wi-Fi, Bluetooth, and Thread. A dedicated <a href="https://fccid.io/2BO3L-TMCTAW5W/Test-Report/TMCTAW5W-FCC-Test-Report-60GHz-R2-9609543.pdf">radio test report</a> also confirms a built-in 60 GHz radar. This could enable presence or approach detection.<br/>
<br/>
According to the leaker known as "<a href="https://x.com/itspdfu/status/2107818771213664728?s=20">pdfu</a>," the thermostat offers a "Far Sight Display" setting, an option to display humidity, and controls for screen brightness and language. The leaker also lists temperature calibration and temperature offset settings, allowing users to adjust its temperature readings.<br/>
<br/>
The <a href="https://fccid.io/2BO3L-TMST2A6W/Label/ID-Label-Location-Info-9489338">temperature sensor</a>, model TMST2A6W, is also round and runs on a 3-volt CR2477 coin battery. On its back, the product name, model number, and manufacturing information curve around the outer edge.<br/>
<br/>
The middle contains the Matter setup code on the left, a battery warning symbol in the center, and a UL certification mark on the right. Its <a href="https://fccid.io/2BO3L-TMST2A6W/Test-Report/Test-Report-9489345">test report</a> confirms Bluetooth Low Energy and Thread connectivity.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/Temperature-Sensor-Feature.png.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1069129" /><br/>
The temperature sensor measures both temperature and humidity, according to <a href="https://x.com/itspdfu/status/2107818774107967561?s=20">pdfu</a>. Its magnetic back allows it to attach directly to metal surfaces, while an included metal plate with adhesive enables mounting on other surfaces. The sensor's battery is replaceable, and it provides alerts when a replacement is needed.<br/>
<br/>
The <a href="https://fccid.io/2BO3L-TMCDD15/Label/6-TMCDD15S-Label-location-9644372">smart deadbolt lock</a> has a tall interior unit with straight sides and curved top and bottom ends. The filings reveal Graphite and Silver versions of the device, with model numbers TMCDD15G and TMCDD15S, respectively.<br/>
<br/>
It runs on four AA batteries, which sit vertically side by side in a compartment in the upper half. Below them, a large, rotating circular section takes up almost the full width of the lower half. The Matter setup code and regulatory text sit above the battery compartment, near the top of the unit. <br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/Smart-Deadbolt-Lock-Feature.jpg" alt="" width="2500" height="1406" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1069127" /><br/>
The lock supports Bluetooth, Thread, near-field communication, and ultra wideband (UWB). An accompanying <a href="https://fccid.io/2BO3L-TMCDD15/Letter/FCC-Waiver-DA-26-905A1-9679061">FCC order</a> explains that it first verifies a compatible device's access credentials over Bluetooth, then uses UWB to determine its position before granting access. This provides more detail about the proximity-based automatic unlocking described in yesterday's report.<br/>
<br/>
According to <a href="https://x.com/itspdfu/status/2107818768827052305?s=20">pdfu</a>, the lock's keypad rotates open, with an included swivel pin allowing movement to be restricted on one side to prevent it from hitting a nearby wall. Its settings include controls for the touchpad's light ring, a "Door Left Audio Alarm," and lock sound volume. The lock also reportedly supports automatic unlocking, tap to unlock, emergency power, and master codes, comes with two mechanical keys, and fits 2.375-inch (60.3 mm) and 2.75-inch (69.9 mm) brackets.<br/>
